# Boundaries

These are enforced by checks in `tools/acme/` and CI. The agent follows them so the checks never have to fire.

## Code boundaries
- **Import direction:** `skimage` and `skimage2` may import `_skimage2`. `_skimage2` must not import `skimage` or `skimage2`. (Upstream convention; enforced by an import-linter contract.)
- **Private internals:** don't import underscore-prefixed modules from another subpackage's internals in new code. Use the subpackage's public API or `_shared`.
- **Dependencies:** runtime dependencies are fixed by `pyproject.toml` `[project] dependencies`. Adding or loosening one requires a human approval (DevOps gate). Don't add it and ask later.
- **Vendored code:** `_vendored/` is third-party code kept as-is. Don't edit it or add to it.
- **No network in library code or tests**, except the existing `skimage.data` fetch path.

## Context boundaries (synthesized Acme policy)
- Work only from this repository and the docs it contains.
- Don't paste code from external sources, other projects, or web search into the library. Licensing and provenance matter here.
- This project uses no MCP servers. Don't call MCP tools on this repository; if a task seems to need one, stop and say which and why. (At Acme, enforcement is Cursor Enterprise's MCP allowlist in Team Settings → MCP Configuration. A project file can't block personal servers.)
- Secrets never go in code, tests, or prompts.

## When a boundary blocks you
Stop and say which boundary and why. Propose the exception for a human to approve. Never work around a check (no `# noqa` or `--no-verify` to get past a boundary failure).
